src/test/stress/REGRESSIONS

Historical bugs this suite is meant to catch
============================================

A stress suite that has never been shown to fail is a suite that proves
nothing.  This file is the list of real, already-fixed bugs in the
CONCURRENTLY commands, taken from the git history, together with the
scenario that ought to catch each one and the result of actually
checking that -- by putting the bug back and running the suite.

Every entry starts at "untested".  An entry is only moved to "catches"
once the reverted tree has been run and failed, with the failure rate
recorded; an entry that survives a long run is marked "MISSED", which is
a gap in the suite, not a footnote.

The list was built by grepping the history for the commands this suite
drives, so it is biased towards bugs someone wrote a commit message
about.  It is not a coverage target: a suite that catches all of these
and nothing else is still only as good as the last bug someone found.

How to test one
===============

  git switch -c revert-<commit> <base>
  git revert --no-commit <commit>        # or hand-reintroduce, see below
  ninja -C build && meson test -C build --suite setup --suite stress

Reverting the fix alone is usually not enough to see a failure at the
default duration -- these are races.  Run the named scenario in a loop
at a higher stressval, and record how often it fails:

  for i in $(seq 20); do
    meson test -C build --suite setup --suite stress <scenario> \
      --test-args ... PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_concurrently=4'
  done

Two practical notes.  Most fixes older than about 2024 no longer revert
cleanly -- the surrounding code has moved -- and have to be
reintroduced by hand; the entry says what the essential change is.  And
several of these bugs are only observable in an assertion build, which
is what the suite should be run under anyway.

A note on what "caught" means
=============================

Three of the checks do the real work here, and it is worth knowing
which one is expected to fire, because that determines what the
scenario has to declare:

  amcheck        bt_index_parent_check(heapallindexed => true) at the
                 end of the run.  This is what finds heap rows missing
                 from an index.  It checks the indexes the scenario
                 declared, and the primary key of each of its tables.
  index_vs_seq   an indexed read and a seqscan of the same snapshot must
                 agree.  Requires the btree_abalance index.
  the invariant  balances, ledger_sum and friends: a wrong answer that
                 the workload itself can observe.

An entry whose scenario declares no btree index is not being checked by
amcheck at all, whatever else it runs.  See "Where the suite falls
short" at the end.

The detector has a history of its own worth knowing about.  Until
1f28982e409, "amcheck: Fix snapshot usage in bt_index_parent_check",
that function fingerprinted the index under SnapshotAny while the index
had been built under an MVCC snapshot, so an index built by CIC could
be reported as missing heap tuples that it was correct to omit.  Since
that is exactly the configuration this suite uses, against a base that
predates it every amcheck failure here is suspect.  Check for that
commit before believing one.


