  e28bb885196  2022-05-31  Revert changes to CONCURRENTLY that "sped up"
                           Xmin advance
      Letting the horizon advance past a CIC/RIC backend lets other
      backends HOT-prune tuples the build still needs, so the index
      misses them.  The revert is the fix; putting the bug back means
      re-applying d9d0762.
      expect: hot_prune (amcheck, and the check that follows each
              rebuild in the run).
      revert: git revert e28bb885196 conflicts.  Hand-applied instead,
              which is four hunks in ComputeXidHorizons(): skip
              PROC_IN_SAFE_IC backends when accumulating
              data_oldest_nonremovable, and give
              catalog_oldest_nonremovable its own accumulation -- its
              own initialization, its own KnownAssignedXids fold, and no
              longer deriving it from the data horizon further down.
              The other half of d9d0762 never was reverted:
              set_indexsafe_procflags() still runs in the build and
              validate phases, so only procarray.c needs touching.
      status: CATCHES, 8 failures in 8 runs at the default duration, as

                ERROR:  heap tuple (30350,29) from table
                "pgbench_accounts" lacks matching index tuple within
                index "pgb_bid_idx"

              on pgb_bid_idx (six runs) and on pgbench_accounts_pkey
              (two), so both the CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY and the
              REINDEX CONCURRENTLY path.  It arrives fast: a failing run
              takes 17 seconds, well inside the first rebuild.  Eight
              runs with the fix restored are clean.

              Nothing caught this before hot_prune existed.  Two things
              were missing, both worth having on their own:

              - No scenario could produce a HOT update on a large
                table.  An update is HOT only when no index covers a
                column it changes, and every scenario declared an index
                on abalance, which is the one column the load moves.  So
                no HOT chain was ever built and none was ever pruned.
                hot_prune indexes bid, which nothing writes.
              - amcheck only ever checked the indexes a scenario
                declared, and a primary key is never one of those, so
                the primary keys reindex_pkey_concurrently rebuilds went
                unverified.  It now resolves them through the catalog.

              Size matters here as much as the load does: a prune only
              costs a build anything if it lands on a page the build has
              not reached yet, so the window is the length of the scan.
              Measured by hand outside the suite, scale 30 with
              fillfactor 50 gave a bad index in two rebuilds out of six;
              the same table at the default fillfactor gave 590k HOT
              updates out of 680k and no bad index in five.

              The branch make-PROC_IN_SAFE_IC-great-again carries
              a4821e227ce, Revert "Revert changes to CONCURRENTLY that
              'sped up' Xmin advance", and on top of it 2337987590e,
              which separates PROC_IN_SAFE_IC from
