Things this suite has produced that are not explained yet.  They are
here rather than in a commit message because an unreproduced failure is
a lead, not a result.

  REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) not getting its final lock under write load

      REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) pgbench_tellers waited for
      AccessExclusiveLock on that table for 185 seconds and then died on
      the lock timeout, with 23 backends queued behind it for
      RowExclusiveLock the whole time.  The workload was thirty clients
      of tpcb_like, whose transactions are a handful of statements and
      commit in milliseconds -- there was no long-running transaction
      for it to be waiting on.

      Reproduce with:

        PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_mode=soak stress_seed=27072026
                       stress_soak_skip=19 stress_soak_count=1
                       stress_concurrently=2'

      which failed once in six here; at the default duration it took a
      whole slice to appear once.  The scenario is pgbench plus the
      generated decorator, standalone, repack_concurrently as the only
      command.  Nothing about it is exotic: the notable part is how
      ordinary it is.

      What is not established is whether this is starvation or simply a
      long wait that would have ended.  A request that has queued should
      be reached once the transactions ahead of it drain, and these
      drain in milliseconds, so 185 seconds wants explaining -- the
      shapes worth ruling out are a lock upgrade that does not queue
      fairly (REPACK holds ShareUpdateExclusiveLock on the table
      already and is asking to upgrade), and a wait that is retried
      rather than queued.  Both would be characteristics of the feature
      rather than bugs in it, but a command that can block every writer
      for three minutes and then give up is worth the REPACK thread
      knowing about either way.

      Related to the entry under "where the suite falls short" about
      commands starved by workloads that never leave a gap, but not the
      same: the others involved a workload that genuinely held locks
      across pauses, and this one does not.

  a backend that went away with nothing in its log

      pgbench: error: client 6 aborted in command 8 (SQL) of script 1;
      perhaps the backend died while processing

