          rebuilt the parent's primary key with REINDEX CONCURRENTLY,
          and a third did the documented partitionwise index build
        - one soak pass, 14 scenario combinations, at stressval 4

      Two sleeps were compiled into the ancestor loop in
      ExecInitPartitionInfo to widen the window, on the theory that the
      leaf's catalog reads see a state the planner's arbiter list does
      not.  The first went next to the injection point inside the loop
      and was useless: on a leaf with one index it fires after the only
      ancestor lookup.  The second went before the loop, which does
      widen the interval between the root's arbiter list and the leaf's
      reads.  Neither produced a sighting, so that theory is neither
      confirmed nor excluded -- only that 2ms of it is not enough.

      The mid-loop hypothesis is now excluded, by experiment rather
      than by argument.  The idea was that the leaf's catalog reads see
      a state the planner's arbiter list does not, because
      get_partition_ancestors() refreshes the catalog snapshot each time
      round the loop (the XXX comment above it says so).  The loop has
      an injection point in it,
      exec-init-partition-after-get-partition-ancestors, so a backend
      can be held there while another session tries the DDL.  Four
      commands were tried, each with lock_timeout so a block shows up as
      an error rather than a hang:

        REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY <partitioned pkey>
                                        ShareLock on the relation
        REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY <leaf pkey>
                                        ShareLock on a virtual xact
        ALTER TABLE .. DETACH PARTITION .. CONCURRENTLY
                                        ShareLock on a virtual xact
        ALTER INDEX .. ATTACH PARTITION <child index>
                                        AccessExclusiveLock on the index

      All four blocked on locks the executing backend already holds, and
      the upsert came out clean every time.  So no catalog change that
      could disturb the mapping can commit while a backend is inside the
      loop; the window this file spent 44 runs and two compiled-in
      sleeps trying to widen does not exist.

      That relocates the race to before the statement takes its locks:
      the arbiter list is decided when the plan is made, and the leaf's
      ancestors are read when the executor first opens that partition.
      A skew between the two has to be established across a statement
      boundary, not inside one -- which fits the scenario that produces
      it, since partitions is the one running force_generic_plan with
      the prepared protocol against an upsert wrapped in a plpgsql
      function, so its plans are cached and revalidated rather than
      built fresh.  The shape to try next: cache the plan in one
      session, let REINDEX CONCURRENTLY on the *partitioned* index
      complete from another while the first sits idle, then execute the
      cached plan.  That rebuild gives the parent index a new OID, so a
      plan still naming the old one would find no leaf index descended
      from it -- matched = 0 against a root list of 1, which is exactly
      the failing arithmetic.

      Something else that was never exercised until 2026-07-27: an
      arbiter list with more than one index in it.  Every ON CONFLICT
